"Where Prefill Expert Reduction Hurts MoE Models:A Few Prompt Tokens Carry Most of the Short-Answer F1 Loss (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22454v1)\nciteturn41205view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn41196view1\",\"lineno\":200}); Total lines: 696\nL156: Table 1: Where the loss is made, by family (HotpotQA, half the experts, earlier unmarked batch; change against the unmodified model with 95% intervals; DeepSeek’s 8–32 comes from the vLLM 0.31 prefix sweep of Section cite15†3.3 ). Qwen1.5’s loss is measured by containment (whether the gold answer appears), since its F1 rises.\nL157: Model  | Uniform reduction  | What is lost  | Where it is made  | First and last 32 full\nL158: --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL159: Qwen3-30B-A3B  | F1 $-$3.34 [$-$4.45, $-$2.26]  | form  | last two tokens  | $-$0.41 [$-$1.23, +0.39]\nL160: DeepSeek-V2-Lite  | F1 $-$8.87 [$-$10.38, $-$7.37]  | form  | first 8–32 tokens (instruction or prompt start)  | +0.95 [$-$0.17, +2.12]\nL161: Qwen1.5-MoE-A2.7B  | containment $-$4.50 (F1 +2.13)  | content  | last two tokens  | containment +0.05 [$-$1.25, +1.35]\nL162: OLMoE-1B-7B  | F1 $-$5.11 [$-$6.76, $-$3.43]  | form (precision)  | both ends, beyond the last two  | $-$0.94 [$-$2.25, +0.34]\nL163: ## 4 Why These Tokens\nL164: ### 4.1 In Qwen3 the boundary is consistent with carrying the instruction to the answer\nL165: Three measurements on the unmodified model fit one account (Figure cite105†3 ). Attention. The query that predicts the first answer token attends to the second-to-last prompt token 15 times more per token than to a question token; attention is not importance, though, since the last token is read three times more than the second-to-last and matters far less when reduced (Section cite14†3.2 ). Path blocking.\nL166: Blocking single attention paths without reducing any expert, the first answer token depends on the last two tokens seeing the instruction (+6.21 nats) and the documents (+3.35), and barely on their seeing the question (+0.43); the stop token depends on the answer seeing the last two prompt tokens (+20.70 nats), not the documents (+0.64) or the question (+0.74). Hiding as many random prompt tokens from the answer costs nothing (Appendix cite34†B ).\nL167: A block removes a heavily attended token outright, a far larger perturbation than reduction (which raises the answer NLL by 1.42 nats in the 200-question batch of Section cite18†4.2 ), so blocking shows what flows through the boundary tokens, not how reduction damages it. Layer repair.\nL168: With the last eight tokens at two experts, restoring all experts in one band of four layers repairs the most, about half, for layers 20–23 and 24–27 (each leaves 46% of the damage), and at most a tenth in the first twelve or last eight layers; three partial repairs make the damage larger (above 100% in Figure cite105†3 d), so mixed reduced and repaired states do not add up monotonically.\nL169: These results are consistent with middle-layer experts writing “how to answer” into the boundary tokens and the answer reading it there, as safety decisions are anchored in template tokens of dense chat models [cite92†22 ]; they do not identify the mechanism uniquely.\nL170: Figure 3: Why the last tokens matter in Qwen3. (a) The account. (b) Attention each token receives from the query that predicts the first answer token (log scale). (c) Increase in the loss of the model’s own answer when one attention path is blocked: the first answer token needs the last two prompt tokens to see the instruction and documents; the stop token needs the answer to see those two tokens.\nL171: (d) Damage left after repairing one band of layers when the last eight tokens run two experts: the mean increase in the answer’s NLL as a share of the increase with no repair, over 300 prompts. A value above 100% means that the partial repair made the loss larger; the 95% prompt-bootstrap interval of this ratio excludes 100% for experts kept in layers 0–3 (120% [103, 141]) and 44–47 (113% [105, 121]) and for the clean state restored in layers 8–11 (126% [107, 148]).\nL172: Repairing one band mixes repaired and reduced computation, a state the model sees under neither condition. The ratio is of means, which a few prompts dominate.\nL173: ### 4.2 Reduction does not disturb the sink statistics we measured\nL174: Sinks coincide with massive activations on the first token and on delimiters [cite106†33 , cite107†13 , cite108†3 , cite109†29 ], and in Qwen3-30B-A3B these are produced by a few routed super experts, whose removal collapses the sinks [cite95†32 ].\nL175: $K\\to k^{\\prime}$ keeps a token’s highest-weight experts, so super experts are unlikely to be removed, but Qwen3’s two sensitive tokens are heavily attended delimiters and could be secondary sinks, so we measured the question directly in a prespecified HuggingFace batch: DeepSeek-V2-Lite and Qwen3-30B-A3B on 200 standard-layout HotpotQA questions each (Appendix cite34†B ; uniform reduction costs $10.3$ F1 points in DeepSeek and an undetectable $-1.06$ [$-4.41$, $+2.33$] in Qwen3 there).\nL176: Super experts are identified as in cite95†Su et al. [32] . Each intervention is scored by the share of the uniform-reduction increase in answer NLL (of the unmodified model’s own answer) that it removes, which is far less noisy than F1 at this size; Qwen3’s rows rest on NLL alone.\nL177: Table 2: Direct tests of sink-related explanations (HuggingFace, 200 HotpotQA questions per model; DeepSeek $6\\to 3$, Qwen3 $8\\to 4$). Change: ratio of the statistic under uniform reduction to the unmodified model, minus one, averaged over layers 1 onward and heads (first token: attention from the last eight prompt positions, hidden norm, largest absolute channel; last eight prompt tokens: attention from the answer, mean norm and largest channel; for DeepSeek these are not its sensitive tokens).\nL178: Recovery: share of the uniform-reduction increase in answer NLL removed, $\\overline{\\mathrm{NLL}_{\\text{unif}}-\\mathrm{NLL}_{\\text{cond}}}/\\overline{\\mathrm{NLL}_{\\text{unif}}-\\mathrm{NLL}_{\\text{orig}}}$, in percent; rules keep 32 (DeepSeek) or 8 (Qwen3) tokens full. Minus position: paired difference to keeping the sensitive positions full (post hoc, except for the two signal rules, for which it is the quantity of the prespecified rule (h)).\nL179: Restorations and random full tokens mix clean and reduced states (Section cite18†4.2 ). 95% bootstrap intervals over questions. Answer NLL rises from 1.10 to 2.64 nats (DeepSeek) and 0.22 to 1.64 (Qwen3).\nL180:  | DeepSeek-V2-Lite  | Qwen3-30B-A3B\nL181: --- | --- | ---\nL182: Is the sink disturbed? (change under uniform reduction)\nL183: ---\nL184: First token: attention / norm / largest channel  | $+0.3$% / $-0.2$% / $-0.1$%  | $+1.5$% / $-3.2$% / $-3.2$%\nL185: Last eight tokens: attention / norm / largest channel  | $-4.3$% / $-4.3$% / $-1.4$%  | $-0.3$% / $-8.3$% / $-10.0$%\nL186: Hide first token from last eight and answer, $\\Delta$NLL (random token)  | $+1.07$ ($+0.00$) nats  | $+40.6$ ($+0.01$) nats\nL187: Super experts (layer/expert); boundary selections removed  | L3/E54, L4/E38; 12.5%  | L1/E68, L2/E92, L3/E82; 10.8%\nL188:  | Recovery  | Minus position  | Recovery  | Minus position\nL189: Keep the sensitive positions full (first 32 / last 8)  | $+22$ [$+10$, $+33$]  | —  | $+49$ [$+30$, $+65$]  | —\nL190: Restore super experts on every reduced token  | $+1$ [$-4$, $+6$]  | $-20$ [$-31$, $-9$]  | $+8$ [$-11$, $+24$]  | $-41$ [$-64$, $-20$]\nL191: Restore massive activations to clean values  | $-4$ [$-9$, $+1$]  | $-25$ [$-36$, $-14$]  | $+6$ [$-10$, $+22$]  | $-43$ [$-64$, $-22$]\nL192: Keep tokens full by super-expert signal  | $+2$ [$-4$, $+7$]  | $-19$ [$-30$, $-9$]  | $+13$ [$-6$, $+30$]  | $-35$ [$-60$, $-12$]\nL193: Keep tokens full by massive-activation signal  | $+5$ [$-3$, $+12$]  | $-17$ [$-30$, $-3$]  | $+4$ [$-14$, $+20$]  | $-44$ [$-69$, $-23$]\nL194: Keep random tokens full  | $+6$ [$0$, $+11$]  | $-16$ [$-27$, $-5$]  | $-14$ [$-33$, $+2$]  | $-63$ [$-86$, $-40$]\nL195: Restore the first token’s keys and values  | $0$ [$-6$, $+6$]  | —  | $-17$ [$-38$, $0$]  | —\nL196: Restore the sensitive positions’ keys and values  | $+19$ [$+8$, $+30$]  | —  | $-353$ [$-527$, $-247$]  | —\nL197: #### The first-token sink statistics are unchanged.\nL198: Both models have a functioning first-token sink—hiding it from the last eight prompt tokens and the answer raises the answer NLL by 1.1 nats in DeepSeek and 40.6 in Qwen3, against nothing for a random token—yet uniform reduction changes its attention mass, norm and largest activation by at most 3.2%; the prespecified rule (a), defined on DeepSeek, reads excluded, and Qwen3’s first-token statistics are within the same band.\nL199: Over Qwen3’s last eight prompt tokens, which include the two delimiters (we did not measure the two separately), the attention the answer pays them is unchanged ($-0.3$%) and their norm falls by 8.3%, inside the $\\pm 10$% band, so rule (b), prespecified on attention and norm, reads excluded; their largest channel, not part of the rule, falls by 10.0% [$-10.2$, $-9.9$], just outside the band, and restoring massive activations removes $+6$% [$-10$, $+22$] of the loss.\nL200: These are layer- and head-averaged scalars; they do not capture changes in the direction of keys and values, and in Qwen3 cleaning the first token’s keys and values raises the NLL ($-17$%), which an undisturbed sink would not predict unless the mixed state itself does harm. In DeepSeek the ten-word layout shows no detectable loss in this batch ($-2.9$ [$-7.7$, $+1.8$] F1; HuggingFace, 200 questions) although first-token statistics change as little as in the standard layout.\nL201: Whether the boundary tokens act as sink-like registers in the unmodified model is a different question that these tests do not answer: hiding Qwen3’s last two prompt tokens from the answer raises its NLL by 23.9 nats, against 40.6 for hiding the first token from the last eight tokens and the answer (Appendix cite34†B ).\nL202: #### Super experts and massive activations account for less than about a quarter of the loss.\nL203: Only 11–13% of the boundary selections of a super expert are removed by $K\\to k^{\\prime}$, so by construction this reduction rarely removes super experts; the tests rule out their removal, not a disturbance of sink mechanisms through other experts. Restoring super experts on every reduced token, or massive activations to their clean values, removes at most 8% of the loss in point estimate; the prespecified rules (c) and (d) read does not explain (upper bound below one half) in both models.\nL204: Because the positional rule itself does not pass that threshold in NLL, the informative comparison is the paired one: both restorations recover 20–43 percentage points less than keeping the sensitive positions full (post hoc). Keeping full the tokens ranked highest by super-expert or massive-activation signals also recovers 17–44 points less than position.\nL205: The prespecified rule (h) calls a signal rule tied with position if the 95% lower bound of this difference exceeds $-25$ percentage points and worse if the upper bound is below $-25$; neither holds in either model, so it reads undecided (DeepSeek’s signal rules are also no better than random tokens, Qwen3’s are); their 32 tokens share one or two with DeepSeek’s first 32, and its F1 stays at the uniform level ($-9.1$ and $-9.6$ against $+1.6$ for the first 32).\nL206: #### The answer NLL is not concentrated where F1 is.\nL207: Splitting the answer NLL (post hoc), in Qwen3 64% of the increase falls on the first answer token, which the last prompt position predicts; keeping the last eight full removes 67% [51, 81] of that part and 8% [$-35$, $+42$] of the increase on later answer tokens, while keeping the whole task interface full (Section cite22†5 ) removes 94% and 89%; the stop token’s share (7%) is too small to estimate.\nL208: In DeepSeek the first 32 tokens remove 75% [63, 86] of the stop token’s increase, 28% of the first answer token’s and none of the later answer tokens’, yet restore F1 fully. The few boundary tokens thus account for most of the short-answer F1 loss but not for most of the likelihood loss, and in Qwen3 much of what they do account for may be the last position’s own reduced computation of the first answer token.\nL209: Separating that writer from the later answer tokens that read the boundary needs the clean-keys-and-values condition, and it fails: cleaning the last eight tokens’ keys and values while their own states stay reduced raises Qwen3’s NLL to 6.68, far above uniform reduction, although unit tests on a small model of the same architecture confirm the patch itself (cleaning every prompt position’s keys and values with the last position kept full reproduces the unmodified model exactly).\nL210: In DeepSeek, whose cleaned tokens do not include the last position, the same condition reproduces keeping the tokens full. Restorations that mix clean and reduced states are not monotone in Qwen3 (random full tokens and clean first-token keys and values also raise the NLL), so we report every such condition (Table cite110†2 ), read none of them as evidence on the boundary channel, and note that a net recovery from a mixed state is not an upper bound on a quantity’s contribution.\nL211: DeepSeek’s two shared experts are never reduced, which may make it less exposed to super-expert loss.\nL212: ## 5 Where the Full-Expert Budget Goes\nL213: The localisation predicts that, at a fixed number of full-expert tokens, where they go decides whether the loss is avoided. We test this on real prompts in which the application marks the documents. Interface-Full reduces only the marked documents and keeps the task interface—system message, instruction, demonstration questions and answers, current question and chat-template tokens—full, on average about 4% of tokens (0.6–26% by task).\nL214: Two controls keep, prompt by prompt, the same number of full tokens: at the two prompt ends (equal-budget ends), or at random positions inside the documents at least 32 tokens from either end, so that the whole interface is reduced (negative control); a third, the inverted control, reduces exactly the interface. Two rules need no marks: template role reduces the user-message content, and KeepEnds keeps a fixed 32+32 tokens.\nL215: All rules share one vLLM 0.31 hook, checked on every prompt against the server’s token ids (Appendix cite58†E ). Table cite111†3 collects the comparisons from a joint quality-and-latency batch (760 prompts) and three prespecified quality-only batches (about 2,100 prompts each; Appendices cite64†G –cite75†J ).\nL216: Table 3: Where the full-expert budget goes (vLLM 0.31, F1 points, task-averaged, 95% bootstrap intervals over questions unless marked 90%). Batches: joint (760 prompts, H100, exploratory), neg. (negative control), interf. (interface), place. (placement; H200), A100 (2,000 long-system HotpotQA questions). Status: pri/sec prespecified primary/secondary, post post hoc; the prespecified equivalence margins were $\\pm 2$. Seven placements of the negative control: one in neg., one in interf., five in place.\nL217: Comparison  | Batch  | Difference  | Status\nL218: Qwen3-30B-A3B, $8\\to 4$\nL219: ---\nL220: Uniform $-$ unmodified  | joint / neg. / place.  | $-3.51$ / $-2.15$ / $-1.83$  | —\nL221: Interface-Full $-$ unmodified  | neg. / place.  | $+0.46$ [$-0.62$, $+1.57$] / $+0.76$ [$-0.49$, $+2.06$]  | sec\nL222:  | joint  | $+0.78$, 90% [$-0.31$, $+1.89$]  | post\nL223: Interface-Full $-$ negative control  | neg.  | $\\mathbf{+3.22}$ [$+1.74$, $+4.73$]  | pri (rule met)\nL224:  | place., five placements  | $\\mathbf{+3.05}$ [$+1.47$, $+4.68$]; per placement $+2.33$ to $+3.88$  | pri (rule met)\nL225:  | interf.  | $+1.63$ [$-0.08$, $+3.29$], Holm $p=0.18$  | sec\nL226: Negative control $-$ uniform  | neg. / place.  | $-0.61$ [$-1.69$, $+0.43$] / $-0.46$ [$-1.42$, $+0.45$]  | post / sec\nL227: Inverted control $-$ unmodified  | neg.  | $-1.86$ [$-3.45$, $-0.33$], Holm $p=0.07$  | sec\nL228: Interface-Full $-$ header only (header full, rest of budget in documents)  | interf.  | $+1.01$ [$-0.61$, $+2.64$], undecided  | pri\nL229: Interface-Full $-$ equal-budget ends  | joint  | $+0.63$ [$-0.54$, $+1.83$]  | post\nL230:  | neg. / interf. / place.  | 90% [$-0.98$, $+0.41$] / [$-0.93$, $+0.65$] / [$-1.07$, $+0.45$]  | post\nL231: Interface-Full $-$ template role  | joint  | $+1.23$ [$-0.59$, $+3.07$]  | post\nL232: KeepEnds (32+32) $-$ unmodified, long system prompt  | A100  | $-1.55$ [$-2.50$, $-0.60$]  | post\nL233: DeepSeek-V2-Lite, $6\\to 3$\nL234: ---\nL235: Instruction between documents: Interface-Full $-$ equal-budget ends  | interf.  | $-1.67$ [$-3.56$, $+0.19$], undecided  | pri\nL236: Standard layout: Interface-Full $-$ negative control  | interf. / place.  | $+7.92$ [$+4.74$, $+11.20$] / $+8.09$ [$+5.05$, $+11.26$]  | post / sec\nL237: same, containment instead of F1  | interf. / place.  | $-3.75$ [$-7.50$, $0.00$] / $-2.38$ [$-5.88$, $+1.12$]  | post\nL238: #### On the interface the budget avoids the loss; scattered in the documents it does not.\nL239: Uniform $8\\to 4$ costs 1.8–3.5 F1 points on Qwen3 in point estimate (the $-1.83$ of the placement batch has an interval touching zero). With the small budget on the interface, Interface-Full stays within one point of the unmodified model in the two prespecified secondary comparisons (95% lower bounds $-0.62$ and $-0.49$; these were prespecified as tests against zero, and the one-point margin is post hoc), and its containment shows no detectable change ($+0.77$ [$-0.90$, $+2.42$] in the joint batch).\nL240: Scattered at random in the documents, the same number of full tokens helps little: the negative control is below Interface-Full by $3.22$ and $3.05$ points in the two prespecified primaries (rule met: at least half of Interface-Full’s gain over uniform reduction) and by $1.63$, with an interval touching zero, in a prespecified secondary, and where uniform reduction was run in the same batch it is at most 0.43–0.45 points above it (95% upper bounds).\nL241: The seven placements come from two prompt sets of 2,098 prompts (1,498 questions shared) on two GPU types; two placements on the same prompts and GPU differ by 1.8 points, so placement variance, which these intervals omit, is as large as question sampling.\nL242: The other two prespecified primaries are undecided: Interface-Full against keeping only the assistant header full and moving the rest of the budget into the documents ($+1.01$ [$-0.61$, $+2.64$]), and DeepSeek with the instruction between documents (below); a fifth, on DeepSeek in the placement batch, was not run because no layout passed its screen.\nL243: Reducing only the interface costs about as much as uniform reduction ($-1.86$ against $-2.15$ in the same batch), although not significantly after Holm correction. Gold paragraphs matter more than distractors (Appendix cite80†K.2 ); we did not test placing the budget there. On DeepSeek the negative control is about 8 F1 points below Interface-Full, but its containment is if anything higher: the gap is answer length, consistent with Section cite13†3.1 .\nL244: Interface-Full itself is not lossless everywhere: on question-first LongBench and code completion it is 1.6–2.4 points (F1; edit similarity for code) below the unmodified model (Supplementary Table S21, Supplement S3.1), and its containment drops on tool-output prompts (Supplement S3.2); we have not located this residual.\nL245: #### Marks, template roles and positions are not distinguished here.\nL246: In the three quality-only batches the equal-budget ends control is within $\\pm 1.1$ points of Interface-Full (90% intervals), and in the joint batch template role differs by $+1.23$ [$-0.59$, $+3.07$], because in our templates the sensitive tokens lie at the ends these rules keep. No margin was prespecified for these comparisons; two of the three 90% intervals lie within $\\pm 1$, the third just outside, and the equal-budget controls take their per-prompt budget from the marks.\nL247: The boundary is also not sufficient: with a 700-token system prompt, a fixed 32+32-token rule loses 1.55 points on 2,000 questions, mostly form, although it keeps the assistant header full, while the equal-budget ends control, whose head of about 300 tokens also stops short of the instruction, loses little (Appendix cite64†G ). In Qwen3, then, form also depends on how much of a long system prompt is reduced, through tokens we have not located.\nL248: The results support a placement principle—keep the template boundary, the system prompt, the instruction and the question full—rather than a better selector. The direct test of marks against positions away from the ends, DeepSeek with the instruction between the documents, was a prespecified primary but is uninformative: that layout shows no loss to recover ($-1.67$, undecided).\nL249: ## 6 What It Buys in Serving\nL250: In the joint batch (one H100, short answers, TTFT one request at a time), the median TTFT of Interface-Full is $0.86$ [$0.86$, $0.87$] of the unmodified model on LongBench prompts [cite112†2 ] (median 10.9K tokens), against 0.85 for uniform reduction, and $1.02$ [$1.01$, $1.03$], slightly slower, on HotpotQA prompts of about 2K tokens, where uniform reduction saves only 4% although the multiply-add model predicts far more (unexplained); the intervals omit start-to-start variation (4%).\nL251: The same implementation is slower than the unmodified model on Qwen3.6-35B-A3B at 1–4K tokens (Appendix cite53†D and Supplement S9). The saving is small and length-dependent, in line with SlimWise [cite91†28 ]; our results say where not to reduce if one does.\nL252: ## 7 Related Work\nL253: #### Fewer experts at inference.\nL254: Static methods remove experts once [cite113†26 , cite114†20 , cite115†21 , cite116†10 ]; dynamic methods skip experts per token by gate weight or frequency [cite113†26 , cite117†16 , cite118†5 , cite119†17 ], allocate them per layer [cite120†7 ], or reduce top-$k$ uniformly; cite121†Chen et al. [6] find that two thirds of the chosen experts suffice, a milder setting than our halving that we did not test.\nL255: ExFold [cite90†36 ] reduces every prompt token and folds the excluded experts into the retained ones with calibrated scalar projections. SlimWise [cite91†28 ] argues that pruning prefill experts costs quality for little throughput, prunes for decoding only, and notes that accuracy can hide pruning-induced changes in generation length, the form effect we locate.\nL256: MC-MoE [cite117†16 ] protects a few important tokens chosen by attention scores (compared in Supplement S3.6); we ask where reduction fails rather than propose a selector.\nL257: #### Sensitive tokens, sinks and super experts.\nL258: Quantization keeps a few pivot or outlier tokens intact [cite122†24 , cite123†4 ], selected by activation magnitude; the tokens we find are the task boundary and are not outliers by the statistics we measured; attention sinks [cite94†37 , cite107†13 , cite108†3 ] coincide with massive activations [cite106†33 , cite109†29 ], which in MoE models a few super experts produce [cite95†32 ].\nL259: In the two models and statistics we measured, prefill expert reduction leaves the first-token sink statistics unchanged and these quantities explain little of the loss; secondary sinks at the boundary tokens are not ruled out.\nL260: #### Where a prompt’s task information sits.\nL261: Final positions aggregate what the output needs [cite124†12 , cite125†15 , cite126†34 ], label words in demonstrations act as anchors [cite127†35 ], and context matters less once the task is recognised [cite128†31 ]; refusal is mediated by a direction read at post-instruction positions [cite93†1 ] and safety decisions are anchored in template tokens [cite92†22 ]. KV-cache and prompt reduction [cite129†23 , cite130†11 , cite131†18 ] keep or drop tokens by similar structural priors; we keep every token and change only its expert budget.\nL262: ## 8 Limitations\nL263: The analysis uses short-answer QA with reasoning off (GSM8K, code completion and reasoning-on runs are checks only), and the loss we localise is measured by F1, which is length-sensitive; in answer NLL the boundary accounts for about half (Qwen3) or a fifth (DeepSeek) of the increase, and with reasoning on uniform reduction showed no detectable loss. We tested only half the experts and $8\\to 2$, not milder settings, and models up to 30B parameters apart from a Qwen3.6 serving check.\nL264: The mechanism is traced only in Qwen3, on unmarked templates, with interventions that do not identify it uniquely and whose partial repairs are not monotone; the last position’s own computation of the first answer token is not separated from what later tokens read. The direct sink tests use 200 questions and averages over layers and heads, which could hide changes in single sink heads; Qwen3’s rows rest on NLL.\nL265: The equal-budget evidence is from Qwen3 and, for the negative control, DeepSeek-V2-Lite; we varied placement, not amount; the sensitive tokens always lay at a prompt end, so marks were never tested where positions fail. Interface-Full leaves an unlocated residual of up to 2.4 points on question-first, tool and code layouts, and a fixed 32+32 rule loses form on long system prompts although it keeps the boundary.\nL266: Latency comes from one GPU (TTFT one request at a time, throughput at concurrency four) without prefix caching.\nL267: ## 9 Conclusion\nL268: Halving the experts of prompt tokens in MoE prefill mostly costs answer form on short-answer QA, and in F1 the cost is made at a few tokens at one end of the prompt: in Qwen3 the template tokens before the answer, in DeepSeek-V2-Lite the start of its standard layout, where the instruction sits; in answer likelihood these positions account for half or less of the damage.\nL269: Reduction leaves the first-token sink statistics we measured nearly unchanged, and restoring super experts or massive activations recovers at most about a quarter of the loss. In our templates and standard layouts a small budget on the task interface avoids the F1 loss and the same budget scattered in the documents does not; rules that keep the prompt ends are not distinguished from rules that read marks.\nL270: ## References\nL271:   * [1] Andy Arditi, Oscar Obeso, Aaquib Syed, Daniel Paleka, Nina Panickssery, Wes Gurnee, and Neel Nanda. Refusal in language models is mediated by a single direction. In Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems, 2024. URL cite132†https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.11717 .\nL272:   * [2] Yushi Bai, Xin Lv, Jiajie Zhang, et al. LongBench: A Bilingual, Multitask Benchmark for Long Context Understanding. arXiv preprint arXiv:2308.14508, 2023. URL cite133†https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.14508 .\nL273:   * [3] Federico Barbero, Álvaro Arroyo, Xiangming Gu, Christos Perivolaropoulos, Michael Bronstein, Petar Veličković, and Razvan Pascanu. Why do LLMs attend to the first token? arXiv preprint arXiv:2504.02732, 2025. URL cite134†https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.02732 .\nL274:   * [4] Mengzhao Chen, Yi Liu, Jiahao Wang, Yi Bin, Wenqi Shao, and Ping Luo. PrefixQuant: Eliminating outliers by prefixed tokens for large language models quantization. arXiv preprint arXiv:2410.05265, 2024. URL cite135†https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.05265 .\nL275:   * [5] Yuanteng Chen, Yuantian Shao, Peisong Wang, and Jian Cheng. EAC-MoE: Expert-Selection Aware Compressor for Mixture-of-Experts Large Language Models. In Proceedings of the Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (ACL), 2025. URL cite136†https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.01625 .\nL276:   * [6] Yuanteng Chen, Qiwei Lai, Chen Tianqi, Peisong Wang, Yuantian Shao, Nanxin Zeng, Zhilei Liu, Chuangyi Li, Jing Liu, and Jian Cheng. You Only Need 2/3 of the Chosen Experts: An Empirical Study of Dynamic Expert Pruning in Fine-Grained MoE LLMs. arXiv preprint arXiv:2609.25809, 2026. URL cite137†https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.25809 .\nL277:   * [7] Krishna Teja Chitty-Venkata, Sandeep Madireddy, Murali Emani, and Venkatram Vishwanath. LExI: Layer-Adaptive Active Experts for Efficient MoE Model Inference. arXiv preprint arXiv:2509.02753, 2025. URL cite138†https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.02753 .\n"
